INT. ABANDONED GRAIN ELEVATOR - NIGHT

Dust hangs in lantern light. Wind moans through the slats. Sacks of rotted feed are stacked into a crude barricade.

HOLLIS BRANDT, 50s, county sheriff, grey mustache, crouches behind the sacks with a Winchester. Beside him, WREN CALLOWAY, 19, deputy, freckled and shaking, thumbs shells into a revolver.

HOLLIS
Count 'em again.

WREN
Same as before. Three on the ladder side.

A shot punches through the slats. Splinters rain on Hollis's hat.

At the far wall, JEB MOSS, 40s, a wiry prisoner in irons, grins from a post he's chained to.

JEB
Sheriff, my brother don't miss twice.

HOLLIS
Then quit talking and give him a reason to miss.

INES VALDEZ, 30s, a rancher's widow in a duster, kneels by the trap door, shotgun across her knees. She does not look up.

INES
They're cutting the rope below us.

WREN
What rope?

INES
The one holding the stairs, son.

The floor lurches. Everyone grabs something. Jeb laughs, rattling his chain.

CUTTER RAINE (O.S.)
Send out Jeb and nobody dies!

Hollis levels the Winchester at the trap door.

HOLLIS
Cutter, I got six men and a hundred rounds.

CUTTER RAINE (O.S.)
You got four and a lamp.

Ines racks the shotgun. Wren swallows.

Boots clatter on the ladder. The trap door flies up. CUTTER RAINE, 30s, black duster, scarred jaw, rises into the light with both hands out.

CUTTER
Hold your fire. I'm coming up to talk.

HOLLIS
Talk from there.

Cutter smiles and keeps climbing.

Behind him, a second figure hauls into view: DOC AMBROSE FINCH, 60s, bald, round spectacles, a medical bag in one hand and a stick of dynamite in the other.

DOC
Evening, all. Nobody move.

Hollis swings the barrel onto Doc. Ines swings hers onto Cutter. Wren points at everything.

JEB
Well, now.

Silence. Wind. The dynamite fuse hisses, lit.
